-- title:
--   Surjectivity of a comparison map between universal deformation rings
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a discrete valuation domain, complete for its maximal-ideal-adic topology, with finite residue field, and let $\bar\rho$ be a residual Galois representation over $k=\mathcal{O}/\mathfrak{m}$, i.e. a $k$-vector space of rank $2$ with an action of $\mathrm{Aut}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ that is trivial on the automorphisms fixing some finite extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ inside $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Let $\mathcal{D}_0$ and $\mathcal{D}'$ be deformation conditions, i.e. predicates on rank-$2$ adically continuous Galois representations over local $\mathcal{O}$-algebras, and let $D_0$, $D'$ be universal deformation data for $\bar\rho$ with these conditions: each consists of a complete noetherian local $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $R$ with local structure map and residue field generated by that of $\mathcal{O}$, a proof that $\bar\rho$ is absolutely irreducible, a representation $\rho$ over $R$ satisfying the condition and residually equivalent to $\bar\rho$ base changed along the residue field map, together with the universal property that any such representation over any complete noetherian local $\mathcal{O}$-algebra is obtained, up to equivalence, by base change along a unique local $\mathcal{O}$-algebra homomorphism from $R$. Let $\theta\colon R'\to R_0$ be a local $\mathcal{O}$-algebra homomorphism such that the base change of $\rho'$ along $\theta$ is equivalent to $\rho_0$. Assume that, for the quotient $R'/\ker\theta$ regarded as a local ring with local quotient map, the base change of $\rho'$ along $R'\to R'/\ker\theta$ satisfies $\mathcal{D}_0$. Then $\theta$ is surjective.
--
--   This is the standard fact, going back to Mazur's theory of deformations of Galois representations, that a morphism of universal deformation rings compatible with the universal representations is surjective, here with the extra hypothesis that the representation over $R'/\ker\theta$ satisfies the target deformation condition (an abstract condition need not be stable under quotients). It is used in the construction of patching data and in the identification of deformation rings with local Hecke algebras in the modularity-lifting steps.
